wasi-sh: host builtins are a name completion can offer.

Depends on ash-hostbuiltin.patch, whose registry is one of the kinds of name a
command can be in this build.

libbb/lineedit.c already knows how to complete a word — applet names, this
shell's builtins, its functions, its aliases, a PATH scan, directory-vs-file,
and an ESC-injection filter on every candidate. It learns them from ash through
ash_command_name(), upstream's enumerator for completion, which walks builtins,
then functions, then aliases.

Host builtins are the FOURTH kind of name find_command() resolves here, and the
only kind the shell cannot enumerate for itself: the registry lives in JS,
behind a lookup(name) that answers one question at a time. Without this,
`php` is a command the shell runs and Tab has never heard of.

The far end is optional. The JS contract's names() is opt-in, because an
embedder may register a lazy namespace with nothing to list; then
host_builtin_name(0) is NULL and the walk simply ends, exactly as it does in a
build with no host builtins at all.

HISTORY, because the shape of what was here explains what is not. This began as
a much larger patch that ALSO exposed the completion engine as a `compgen`
command, for "a terminal that edits its lines in the host" — and its stated
reason was that wide's guest "has no choice, because it is parked on a
/dev/hostreq verb loop". That was a choice, not a constraint, and it was
unwound: the guest is a terminal now, its own Tab is the completion, and the
command nothing typed is gone. What is left is the arm that was always real.

--- a/shell/ash.c
+++ b/shell/ash.c
@@ -8813,6 +8813,13 @@
  *                                    own exceptions and returns -1). */
 extern int host_builtin_lookup(const char *name);
 extern int host_builtin_run(char **argv, char **envp);
+/*   host_builtin_name(i)          -> the i'th registered name, or NULL past the
+ *                                    end (and always NULL if the embedder did
+ *                                    not implement the optional names()).
+ *                                    Points into a static buffer, valid until
+ *                                    the next call — every caller here xstrdup's
+ *                                    it immediately. */
+extern const char *host_builtin_name(int i);
 
 /*
  * Resolve a command name.  If you change this routine, you may have to
@@ -11430,6 +11526,24 @@
 	}
 #endif
 
+	/* wasi-sh: host builtins are the fourth kind of name find_command() can
+	 * resolve here, and the only kind a host cannot enumerate for itself —
+	 * the registry lives in JS, behind a lookup(name) that answers one
+	 * question at a time. Without this arm, `php` is a command the shell runs
+	 * and completion has never heard of.
+	 *
+	 * Optional at the far end: the contract's names() is opt-in, because an
+	 * embedder may register a lazy namespace with nothing to list. Then this
+	 * returns NULL at index 0 and the list simply ends, which is also what a
+	 * build with no builtins at all does. */
+	for (n = 0;; n++) {
+		const char *hb = host_builtin_name(n);
+		if (!hb)
+			break;
+		if (--i < 0)
+			return hb;
+	}
+
 	return NULL;
 }
 #endif
